None of the other libraries the wheel's files need at run time is carried: not
the system's glibc (`libc.so.6`, `libpthread.so.0`), and not the codec
libraries libz, liblz4, libzstd, libbz2 and liblzma, which komira opens by name
from the host when a file uses that codec. The libraries it does not carry
include no aws-lc, s2n-tls or other TLS library: none is linked.

The Mojo runtime: a dependency, not distributed
-----------------------------------------------

komira's libraries, the shared library libkomira among them, are compiled
by Modular's Mojo compiler and need the Mojo runtime libraries when they are
loaded (libKGENCompilerRTShared, libAsyncRTRuntimeGlobals and
libMSupportGlobals; .so on Linux, .dylib on macOS). These libraries are
Modular's, are not part of this repository, and are not covered by its
Apache License grant.

The Python package `komira` and komira's conda packages are built to carry
none of these libraries and no other file of Modular's. Each declares a
dependency on Modular's own `mojo-compiler` package at the compiler version
the libraries were built with, and the user's package installer fetches that
package from Modular's publication (PyPI for the wheel, Modular's conda
channel for the conda packages) under the license that package declares. On
PyPI, `mojo-compiler` 1.0.0 declares `LicenseRef-MAX-Platform-Software-License`.
docs/design/mojo_runtime_pypi_vs_conda.md records what that package holds.
The wheel carries this file as one of its two license files; a conda package
carries only LICENSE.

This section does not cover `mojo_bundle` outputs (tools/build/package), such
as the base image under packaging/images: a bundle does carry the Mojo runtime
libraries in its `lib/`.
